hn_id: '50591853'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50591853
image: /infographics/01-github-50591853.jpg
---

Standard weight quantization algorithms treat model layers with uniform coarse heuristics, leading to severe perplexity degradation at sub-four-bit precision. LookAtMyQuant takes a compiler-driven approach, measuring the exact Kullback-Leibler divergence penalty of compressing each individual tensor component before assigning bit allocations.

By mapping sensitivity across parameter blocks, the engine allocates representation density strictly where precision loss would corrupt token generation. This targeted allocation achieves twelve times lower divergence than standard Q4_K_M at identical file footprints, enabling larger architectures to execute cleanly in five gigabytes of memory.

For infrastructure engineers deploying local inference pipelines, selective bit allocation turns quantization from a blunt truncation exercise into a fine-grained optimization problem.

Model compression should be governed by mathematical sensitivity rather than arbitrary bit boundaries.

