title: Upstream HTTP/1.1 is inherently insecure and requires HTTP/2
source: hn
url: https://http1mustdie.com/
date: '2026-11-21'
authors:
- PortSwigger
why_read: Read this to understand why request demarcation ambiguity in upstream HTTP/1.1 enables severe desync attacks across modern CDNs. You will learn how deploying upstream HTTP/2 eliminates these vulnerabilities and how to defend existing infrastructure.
hn_id: '50586540'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50586540
image: /infographics/04-hn-50586540.jpg
---

Upstream HTTP/1.1 remains one of the largest architectural liabilities across modern web infrastructure. Even when edge reverse proxies terminate client traffic over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3, continuing to speak HTTP/1.1 to upstream origin services routinely introduces catastrophic request smuggling vulnerabilities.

The core issue stems from protocol ambiguity. Parsing request boundaries in HTTP/1.1 relies on Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding headers, which disparate proxies, load balancers, and application servers interpret with subtle discrepancies. Attackers exploit these edge cases through novel desync vectors, allowing them to poison reverse proxy connection pools, bypass authentication, and hijack arbitrary customer sessions across major content delivery networks.

Mitigating this risk at the edge through regex normalization or header filtering has proven fragile across six years of defensive patching. The definitive solution is enforcing end-to-end HTTP/2 across your internal infrastructure. Because HTTP/2 defines unambiguous binary framing for request multiplexing, boundary confusion between reverse proxies and origin servers becomes structurally impossible.

If you manage backend systems behind reverse proxies, auditing and upgrading your upstream transport layer should be your next priority.

