title: Compacting Postgres tables concurrently without application downtime
source: hn
url: https://www.enterprisedb.com/blog/squeezing-tables-postgres-19
date: '2026-11-21'
authors:
- Álvaro Herrera
why_read: Read this to understand why standard VACUUM FULL blocks table writes during bloat cleanup and how Postgres approaches concurrent compaction.
hn_id: '50587194'
comments: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50587194
image: /infographics/09-hn-50569903.jpg
---

Every Postgres operator dreads table bloat on high-velocity production tables. While standard vacuum frees dead tuples within existing pages, it cannot compact empty space stranded across the middle of a relation.

For years, the nuclear option was VACUUM FULL. It rewrites the entire table cleanly into a transient relation and swaps storage files, but it holds an exclusive table lock. If you run it on a terabyte-scale table supporting an active service, your application is effectively offline during the rewrite. Third-party tools like pg_squeeze solved this with logical decoding, but managing external extensions in managed database environments remained painful.

Postgres 19 brings concurrent compaction patterns natively into core. By decoupling page-level tuple migration from exclusive table locks, the engine allows active reads and writes to proceed while background workers repack relation pages.

Zero-downtime storage reclamation should be an engine primitive, not an operational tightrope walk.

