When large language models generate massive volumes of production code, raw development velocity increases, but comprehension drops precipitously. Systems quickly drift into an unmaintainable state where developers stop reasoning about architecture and instead apply probabilistic patches until the test suite passes.

Designing for understandability must become as formal a discipline as designing for testability. Without deliberate boundaries, automated generation simply acts as an accelerator for technical debt. Engineering teams cannot review generated diffs effectively if interfaces do not enforce strict structural rules.

The solution requires resurrecting rigorous software principles: explicit design by contract, enforced class invariants, and modular information hiding. Invariants ensure that an object cannot enter an invalid state regardless of what code path generated the update. Contracts provide deterministic preconditions and postconditions that both human reviewers and language models can verify without holding entire call trees in memory.

Judgment of software architecture remains the core responsibility of senior engineers. Accelerating code generation without enforcing system comprehensibility only builds brittle monoliths faster.

Treat comprehensibility as an architectural constraint rather than an afterthought.

