Most attempts to adapt frontier language models to robotics depend on costly human teleoperation datasets and heavy custom harnesses. A new research effort demonstrates that open 27B reasoning models can learn physical manipulation through reinforcement learning entirely within simulated environments.

The framework, titled Robot-Use RL, treats a robot arm analogously to how computer-use models interact with an operating system. The model ingests multimodal sensory input, produces an explicit chain of thought, and directly emits the arm's trajectory without any middleman harness.

Evaluated on the LIBERO benchmark, the approach improved success rates from 1.6 percent to 74.4 percent. More remarkably, policy execution on a real dual-arm physical robot went from 0 percent to 60 percent without fine-tuning on physical robot demonstrations, all while retaining the model's base coding and reasoning scores.

Zero-shot transfer from simulated RL to real-world hardware proves that end-to-end reasoning can generalize across physical actuators without brittle hand-coded wrappers.

