---

Activation checkpointing sounds simple in theory: drop intermediate tensors during the forward pass and recompute them during backward execution to conserve GPU memory. In distributed training harnesses like TorchTitan, however, naive recomputation breaks down quickly because forward passes involve collective communication, side effects, and non-deterministic routing.

When integrating torch_remat into TorchTitan, PyTorch engineers discovered that running backward recomputation often meant executing communication collectives twice. Without explicit region boundaries, distributed models like Mixture-of-Experts would rerun routing counters and trigger duplicate side effects during replay. Even worse, the lack of an early-stop optimization caused recomputation to consume more memory than simply stashing the original tensors.

The solution required replacing aten-level selective activation checkpointing policies with named semantic regions in model code. Assigning dedicated regions to collectives and explicitly pinning down state updates ensures that replay behaves deterministically across ranks.

Effective large model infrastructure requires treating memory optimization and distributed communication as tightly coupled problems rather than independent layers.
